So Bob, why don't you do a few snowplow turns?

62
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:09,840
Gnarly run, Rick.

63
00:03:10,280 --> 00:03:10,760
Nice carving.

64
00:03:11,560 --> 00:03:12,620
You've got some mad skills.

65
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:13,440
That was sick.

66
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:14,860
You want to see gnarly?

67
00:03:15,380 --> 00:03:16,400
Well, see that bump over there?

68
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:20,600
I'm going to catch some major air.

69
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:21,600
Butt plant.

70
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:22,600
Ha ha ha.

71
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:23,350
He lost his skis.

72
00:03:23,770 --> 00:03:24,330
Yard sale.

73
00:03:24,690 --> 00:03:26,750
Well, thanks for joining us here today.

74
00:03:27,310 --> 00:03:28,290
I think that about does it.

75
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:31,600
Bob, Ian, time for some après-ski?

76
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:32,210
No way, man.

77
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:43,360
We're off to grab some freshies.

78
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:45,360
Pretty embarrassing for Rick there, wasn't it?

79
00:03:45,660 --> 00:03:49,480
Yeah, he was a little bit cocky and then he ended up falling.

80
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:54,280
Well, I think there's a lesson in that for all of us, but what the real lesson is today is language.

81
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:56,680
So why don't we start with language takeaway?

82
00:03:58,580 --> 00:03:59,500
Language takeaway.

83
00:04:00,040 --> 00:04:04,280
All right, on language takeaway today, let's take a look at the first word.

84
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:05,280
We have some bindings.

85
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:05,480
Bindings.

86
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:06,880
Bindings.

87
00:04:07,880 --> 00:04:11,320
Bindings are sort of like a lock that attaches your boot to your ski.

88
00:04:12,020 --> 00:04:13,500
So those are the bindings.

89
00:04:13,780 --> 00:04:16,460
I guess it comes from the verb to bind.

90
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:18,820
Yeah, to tie something so that it stays.

91
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:19,560
Okay.

92
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:20,640
Binding.

93
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:23,080
So for example, my bindings are broken.

94
00:04:23,640 --> 00:04:23,800
Okay.

95
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:29,050
And another piece of skiing equipment and very important is goggles.

96
00:04:29,350 --> 00:04:29,750
Goggles.

97
00:04:30,290 --> 00:04:31,870
Okay, we have goggles now.

98
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:33,200
Yep.

99
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:34,560
Not to be confused with Google.

100
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:35,990
It's spelled almost the same way, huh?

101
00:04:36,440 --> 00:04:37,440
Yep.

102
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:42,280
But goggles are kind of like, imagine a big pair of glasses that you wear when you ski.

103
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:44,040
So it makes people kind of look like flies.

104
00:04:44,580 --> 00:04:44,840
Yes.

105
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:46,240
They have these huge glasses.

106
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:47,240
Yeah.

107
00:04:47,240 --> 00:04:55,560
And you'll also see safety goggles, which you might wear if you are a mechanic fixing a car or someone working with wood or chemicals or what have you.

108
00:04:55,560 --> 00:05:00,080
Okay, so goggles basically protect your eyes from snow, from the sun, from anything.

109
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:00,780
That's right.

110
00:05:01,640 --> 00:05:01,800
All right.

111
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:04,340
So moving on to our next ski word.

112
00:05:04,840 --> 00:05:09,800
They said you should wear the goggles so that you don't accidentally go over the moguls.

113
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:11,100
Okay, moguls.

114
00:05:11,480 --> 00:05:14,920
It sounds kind of strange.

115
00:05:14,920 --> 00:05:16,800
What are these things?

116
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:19,960
Basically these are the bumps that are in a ski hill.

117
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:20,820
Bumps.

118
00:05:20,940 --> 00:05:23,440
Okay, so they're kind of like mini little hills.

119
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:24,960
Yep.

120
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:26,870
And they're very difficult to ski over, right?

121
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:28,200
That's right.

122
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:30,080
You got to be pretty good to handle those moguls.

123
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:30,330
Okay.

124
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:34,890
Now, since these guys are beginner skiers, they're starting out on the bunny hill.

125
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:36,190
A bunny hill.

126
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:37,050
The bunny hill.

127
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:37,850
Okay.

128
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:39,490
Very popular among skiers.

129
00:05:39,760 --> 00:05:42,120
The bunny hill is the easiest hill.

130
00:05:42,120 --> 00:05:44,440
So it's where the beginners start to learn.

131
00:05:44,590 --> 00:05:45,190
Yeah, all the kids.

132
00:05:45,530 --> 00:05:46,910
All the kids are on the bunny hill.

133
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:48,560
All right.

134
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:48,890
And our final words.

135
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:53,370
Actually, we're going to look at two words here because they mean almost exactly the same thing.

136
00:05:54,160 --> 00:05:56,880
We heard the ski instructor say gnarly.

137
00:05:58,080 --> 00:05:58,380
Gnarly run.

138
00:05:59,080 --> 00:05:59,600
Yeah, gnarly.

139
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:01,000
So an interesting word.

140
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:04,200
It's spelled G-N-A-R-L-E-Y.

141
00:06:04,780 --> 00:06:06,800
Okay, but you don't say gnarly.

142
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:07,800
No.

143
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:08,800
You say gnarly.

144
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:09,800
Okay.

145
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:11,360
And a related word, sick.

146
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:11,940
Okay.

147
00:06:12,220 --> 00:06:14,420
Now, sick means to be sick, right?

148
00:06:14,520 --> 00:06:15,680
No, no, no.

149
00:06:15,680 --> 00:06:15,820
Not here.

150
00:06:16,680 --> 00:06:18,100
Let's listen to some examples to help us understand.

151
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:20,220
Example one.

152
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:24,380
Those were some sick dance moves.

153
00:06:24,880 --> 00:06:25,320
You are amazing.

154
00:06:28,280 --> 00:06:28,570
Example two.

155
00:06:30,760 --> 00:06:34,270
Jack and I went to the beach yesterday and surfed some really gnarly waves.

156
00:06:36,960 --> 00:06:37,340
Example three.

157
00:06:39,910 --> 00:06:42,070
Man, the concert last night was sick.

158
00:06:42,570 --> 00:06:44,310
I can't believe we got front row seats.

159
00:06:46,470 --> 00:06:46,930
Okay.

160
00:06:46,930 --> 00:06:51,070
So, basically, gnarly and sick, they mean that it's amazing, it was cool.

161
00:06:51,330 --> 00:06:51,750
It's awesome.

162
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:53,400
Awesome.

163
00:06:53,400 --> 00:06:54,400
Yeah.

164
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:56,280
So, these two words are very, very informal.

165
00:06:56,280 --> 00:06:56,890
It's slang.

166
00:06:57,280 --> 00:06:58,280
Yeah.

167
00:06:58,280 --> 00:06:59,070
And actually, skier slang.

168
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:03,610
Both of these words, I think, originated with skiers, snowboarders, skateboarders.

169
00:07:04,790 --> 00:07:05,210
Gnarly dudes.

170
00:07:05,240 --> 00:07:06,240
Gnarly dudes.

171
00:07:06,240 --> 00:07:07,240
So, okay.

172
00:07:07,240 --> 00:07:14,880
So, that's all the words we have for language takeaway, but we have some great phrases that we want to take a look at before we listen to the dialogue again.

173
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:16,970
So, it's time now for Fluency Builder.

174
00:07:18,210 --> 00:07:19,070
Fluency Builder.

175
00:07:20,010 --> 00:07:20,290
All right.

176
00:07:20,840 --> 00:07:23,040
So, Rick said that it is time to...

177
00:07:23,040 --> 00:07:27,320
So, Rick invited everyone to start skiing by saying, let's hit the slopes.

178
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:28,280
Okay.

179
00:07:28,340 --> 00:07:29,620
So, to hit the slopes.

180
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:30,600
To hit the slopes.

181
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:31,620
Okay.

182
00:07:31,660 --> 00:07:34,080
Now, we want to hit the slopes.

183
00:07:34,340 --> 00:07:34,820
What does that mean?

184
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:36,440
Let's go skiing.

185
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:36,640
Let's go skiing.

186
00:07:37,440 --> 00:07:37,700
Let's go to the mountain.

187
00:07:38,440 --> 00:07:39,440
Yep.

188
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:42,680
Now, we can change the noun slope for something else, right?

189
00:07:42,680 --> 00:07:43,680
Yeah.

190
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:45,320
You can change it for a lot of other different nouns.

191
00:07:46,240 --> 00:07:48,760
And why don't we hear some examples of how you can do that?

192
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:52,800
Example one.

193
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:55,680
I really have to hit the books and study for my exam tomorrow.

194
00:07:57,960 --> 00:08:00,240
Example two.

195
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:01,280
I'm really tired.

196
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:04,800
I think I'm going to hit the bed now.

197
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:06,960
Example three.

198
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:10,920
Honey, can we hit the supermarket on our way home?

199
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:12,920
I need to buy some milk.

200
00:08:13,770 --> 00:08:14,450
Okay, great.

201
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:18,410
So, great examples and now we understand how we can use the hit the something.

202
00:08:19,170 --> 00:08:19,440
All right.

203
00:08:20,090 --> 00:08:20,870
What about our next phrase?

204
00:08:21,290 --> 00:08:25,190
Well, Rick warned Bob that he might take a few spills.

205
00:08:25,890 --> 00:08:26,090
Okay.

206
00:08:26,130 --> 00:08:27,230
So, to take a spill.

207
00:08:27,450 --> 00:08:28,150
To take a spill.

208
00:08:28,550 --> 00:08:29,570
Take a spill.

209
00:08:30,070 --> 00:08:30,250
Fall.

210
00:08:30,880 --> 00:08:31,050
To fall.

211
00:08:31,880 --> 00:08:32,880
Yep.

212
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:33,880
It means like a light fall or...

213
00:08:34,110 --> 00:08:35,190
Yeah, a pretty dramatic fall.

214
00:08:35,440 --> 00:08:35,690
Pretty dramatic.

215
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:37,440
So, you fell.

216
00:08:37,440 --> 00:08:38,440
Yeah.

217
00:08:38,440 --> 00:08:47,880
So, for example, instead of saying, that little girl was riding her bike and she fell, I can say, that little girl was riding her bike and she took a bad spill.

218
00:08:47,880 --> 00:08:48,880
Yep.

219
00:08:48,880 --> 00:08:49,880
Okay.

220
00:08:49,880 --> 00:08:50,040
So, to take a spill.

221
00:08:50,880 --> 00:08:51,640
You have to use the verb take though.

222
00:08:51,880 --> 00:08:52,320
Yes, exactly.

223
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:53,560
You cannot just spill.

224
00:08:54,180 --> 00:08:54,480
Okay.

225
00:08:54,800 --> 00:08:55,720
You have to take a spill.

226
00:08:56,620 --> 00:08:56,980
All right.

227
00:08:57,180 --> 00:08:57,940
What about our next phrase?

228
00:08:58,400 --> 00:09:02,220
Well, you might take a spill when you catch some major air.

229
00:09:02,740 --> 00:09:02,940
Okay.

230
00:09:03,680 --> 00:09:04,680
To catch some major air.

231
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:05,800
To catch some major air.

232
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:07,930
So, am I catching something?

233
00:09:08,410 --> 00:09:09,630
How am I catching air?

234
00:09:09,630 --> 00:09:10,170
That's impossible.

235
00:09:10,590 --> 00:09:13,350
Well, imagine if you're jumping and your skis are catching the air.

236
00:09:13,590 --> 00:09:14,210
Ah, okay.

237
00:09:14,490 --> 00:09:17,350
So, I'm in the air for a very long time maybe?

238
00:09:17,730 --> 00:09:17,910
Yeah.

239
00:09:18,190 --> 00:09:21,730
When you catch some air, basically, it's like you're flying for a couple of seconds.

240
00:09:22,010 --> 00:09:23,170
So, you're flying for a couple of seconds.

241
00:09:23,310 --> 00:09:24,490
You're very high up in the air.

242
00:09:24,760 --> 00:09:25,760
Yep.

243
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:26,760
Okay.

244
00:09:26,760 --> 00:09:27,760
To catch some air.

245
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:28,760
Yeah.

246
00:09:28,760 --> 00:09:41,960
And you'll hear this phrase when you're, you know, not only when you're talking about skiing, but also maybe if you're, you know, you're driving your car really fast and it sort of jumps up in the air for a minute, well, not a minute, a second, that would be catching air as well.

247
00:09:41,960 --> 00:09:42,960
Okay.

248
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:43,960
So, to catch air.

249
00:09:43,960 --> 00:09:44,440
All right.

250
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:45,400
So, they finished skiing.

251
00:09:45,960 --> 00:09:48,560
They were kind of tired and he said, well, let's call it a day.

252
00:09:48,880 --> 00:09:49,720
To call it a day.

253
00:09:50,360 --> 00:09:51,140
Let's call it a day.

254
00:09:51,720 --> 00:09:51,980
Let's go home.

255
00:09:52,800 --> 00:09:53,060
Let's go home.

256
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:54,800
It's over.

257
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:55,340
Let's finish.

258
00:09:55,800 --> 00:09:56,800
Let's finish the day.

259
00:09:56,800 --> 00:09:57,800
Yep.

260
00:09:57,800 --> 00:09:58,800
Let's call it a day and go home.

261
00:09:58,800 --> 00:10:02,800
And not before we go to our next word, to grab some freshies.

262
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:03,640
All right.

263
00:10:04,280 --> 00:10:05,520
Grab us some freshies.

264
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:06,140
Grab some freshies.

265
00:10:06,640 --> 00:10:06,860
Okay.

266
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:07,740
Freshies.

267
00:10:08,520 --> 00:10:08,940
